1
00:00:32,867 --> 00:00:33,826
Open the door!

2
00:00:34,410 --> 00:00:35,661
You heard me!

3
00:00:37,538 --> 00:00:39,999
You little brat, your old man's here.

4
00:00:43,586 --> 00:00:44,462
You little punk.

5
00:01:04,774 --> 00:01:05,941
You little punk.

6
00:01:06,776 --> 00:01:08,110
How dare you lock me out?

7
00:01:08,194 --> 00:01:09,236
I'm sorry.

8
00:01:09,945 --> 00:01:11,447
It's okay.

9
00:01:14,074 --> 00:01:14,950
You bastard.

10
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:16,994
I'm sorry.

11
00:01:17,077 --> 00:01:18,329
What did you say?

12
00:01:18,412 --> 00:01:19,622
I'm sorry.

13
00:01:19,705 --> 00:01:21,332
You're sorry?

14
00:01:21,415 --> 00:01:23,250
Do you know what you did wrong?

15
00:01:23,334 --> 00:01:24,210
Do you?

16
00:01:24,794 --> 00:01:28,214
God knows what
your damned runaway mother is doing,

17
00:01:28,297 --> 00:01:30,758
while all you do at home is eat and sleep.

18
00:01:31,425 --> 00:01:32,593
I'm sorry.

19
00:01:33,511 --> 00:01:35,888
Look at how chubby you've become.

20
00:01:35,971 --> 00:01:38,891
You must be living an easy life,
you little brat.

21
00:01:41,769 --> 00:01:42,728
Damn it.

22
00:02:12,925 --> 00:02:13,926
You little rat.

23
00:02:15,845 --> 00:02:17,763
Open up while I'm being nice.

24
00:02:17,847 --> 00:02:19,431
Open up, you bastard!

25
00:02:22,268 --> 00:02:23,561
Open the door!

26
00:02:25,354 --> 00:02:26,772
Cha Woo-jin!

27
00:02:29,483 --> 00:02:31,360
Open it already, you little punk!

28
00:02:32,027 --> 00:02:33,737
Open the damn door!

29
00:02:34,405 --> 00:02:35,281
Open it!

30
00:02:37,741 --> 00:02:38,576
Open up!

31
00:02:39,410 --> 00:02:41,704
Open the door! Open it!

32
00:02:57,177 --> 00:02:58,053
Open…

33
00:02:59,096 --> 00:02:59,930
Hurry…

34
00:03:01,181 --> 00:03:02,057
Open up…

35
00:03:02,892 --> 00:03:04,351
You damned brat.

36
00:03:04,435 --> 00:03:05,436
Open the door now.

37
00:03:57,446 --> 00:03:59,031
You're okay, Chris.

38
00:04:07,414 --> 00:04:08,499
My goodness.

39
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:11,251
Look at all this sweat.

40
00:04:12,503 --> 00:04:15,506
Stay seated. We called emergency services.

41
00:04:15,589 --> 00:04:16,423
I'm all right.

42
00:04:16,507 --> 00:04:17,800
I'm just anemic, that's all.

43
00:04:18,425 --> 00:04:19,510
All the tension hit me hard.

44
00:04:19,593 --> 00:04:21,220
It didn't seem like simple anemia.

45
00:04:21,303 --> 00:04:24,390
Let's get you to a hospital for a checkup.

46
00:04:31,522 --> 00:04:32,690
I'm really okay.

47
00:04:41,949 --> 00:04:44,910
Sorry for the trouble, Chairwoman.

48
00:05:08,267 --> 00:05:11,478
I dreamed of a peacock two nights ago.

49
00:05:11,562 --> 00:05:13,856
Was it a pheasant and not a peacock?

50
00:05:13,939 --> 00:05:15,941
Come on, give me something.

51
00:05:33,083 --> 00:05:34,960
Guess it was a peacock, after all.

52
00:05:59,234 --> 00:06:00,569
THE TABLE ASSISTANT DIRECTOR

53
00:06:00,652 --> 00:06:03,739
"This mission will take place
away from the filming studio."

54
00:06:04,364 --> 00:06:05,324
"It'll be a long drive,

55
00:06:05,407 --> 00:06:08,202
but we're traveling to a place
with clean water and clear air."

56
00:06:08,285 --> 00:06:11,705
"All teams are to pack
for a five-day business trip."

57
00:06:11,789 --> 00:06:15,250
"Next Wednesday at 9 a.m."

58
00:06:15,959 --> 00:06:17,878
"Meet us at the back door
of the broadcasting station."

59
00:06:19,379 --> 00:06:20,297
Five days?

60
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:22,508
So we have to close the restaurant
for five days?

61
00:06:22,591 --> 00:06:24,468
It's not like they'll compensate us.

62
00:06:24,551 --> 00:06:28,097
Right. What kind of mission
takes five days?

63
00:06:29,056 --> 00:06:30,057
This is too much.

64
00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:32,184
The other teams will complain too.

65
00:06:41,944 --> 00:06:42,778
Chef Oh.

66
00:06:42,861 --> 00:06:43,821
Yes, Chef.

67
00:06:43,904 --> 00:06:46,698
You'll be in charge of Exception
for those five days.

68
00:06:47,324 --> 00:06:48,450
Pardon?

69
00:06:49,576 --> 00:06:51,203
-Michael.
-Yes, Chef.

70
00:06:51,286 --> 00:06:52,788
Pick a garde-manger to fill in.

71
00:06:52,871 --> 00:06:53,789
Sorry?

72
00:06:54,957 --> 00:06:56,083
Yes, Chef.

73
00:07:04,049 --> 00:07:05,843
THE TABLE ASSISTANT DIRECTOR

74
00:07:05,926 --> 00:07:07,636
Business is already slow enough,

75
00:07:07,719 --> 00:07:10,556
and they want us to close for five days?

76
00:07:12,516 --> 00:07:13,475
What will you do?

77
00:07:15,144 --> 00:07:17,938
I don't know. Closing for five long days?

78
00:07:19,022 --> 00:07:20,190
What should we do?

79
00:07:29,575 --> 00:07:30,409
Chris.

80
00:07:34,454 --> 00:07:35,581
Chris.

81
00:07:35,664 --> 00:07:36,498
Huh?

82
00:07:42,379 --> 00:07:44,590
Sorry. I need to get some rest upstairs.

83
00:07:57,102 --> 00:08:00,147
He's had no energy
since we got back from filming yesterday.

84
00:08:01,190 --> 00:08:02,357
Is something wrong?

85
00:08:34,014 --> 00:08:36,350
Han, wake up. Han.

86
00:08:38,352 --> 00:08:40,687
What is it, Dad? I'm sleepy.

87
00:08:40,771 --> 00:08:42,898
We need to pack up and leave.
There's no time.

88
00:08:42,981 --> 00:08:46,151
What? Why? Are we going somewhere again?

89
00:08:46,735 --> 00:08:48,946
They found us. They're coming.

90
00:08:51,114 --> 00:08:52,491
Get dressed. Hurry.

91
00:08:58,080 --> 00:08:58,956
Dad.

92
00:09:02,834 --> 00:09:04,211
Dad.

93
00:09:09,174 --> 00:09:10,259
Han.

94
00:09:10,342 --> 00:09:11,301
Han!

95
00:09:12,302 --> 00:09:14,680
-Han, wake up.
-Wake up.

96
00:09:15,347 --> 00:09:16,807
Please wake up.

97
00:09:17,391 --> 00:09:18,225
Kang Han!

98
00:09:18,308 --> 00:09:19,643
Chris, wake up.

99
00:09:19,726 --> 00:09:20,602
-Han.
-Chris.

100
00:09:20,686 --> 00:09:21,520
Kang Han.

101
00:09:21,603 --> 00:09:22,479
Wake up, Chris!

102
00:09:22,562 --> 00:09:24,231
-Kang Han!
-Chris!

103
00:09:24,314 --> 00:09:25,565
-Han.
-Chris.

104
00:09:25,649 --> 00:09:27,359
-Han!
-Chris, wake up.

105
00:09:27,442 --> 00:09:30,070
Can you hear me? Chris!

106
00:09:30,737 --> 00:09:32,072
Come on, Chris. Wake up.

107
00:09:32,864 --> 00:09:34,032
Chris!

108
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:42,624
You'll die too if you stay here.

109
00:09:42,708 --> 00:09:44,001
-Get out of here!
-No, Dad!

110
00:09:44,084 --> 00:09:45,627
Listen to me now.

111
00:09:46,503 --> 00:09:47,587
Go!

112
00:09:47,671 --> 00:09:49,339
Pyeongyeon killed my father.

113
00:09:49,423 --> 00:09:51,258
Pyeongyeon Group killed him.

114
00:09:51,341 --> 00:09:54,428
They fabricated everything
and drove him to take his own life.

115
00:09:54,511 --> 00:09:56,096
Pyeongyeon killed Dad.

116
00:09:56,179 --> 00:09:58,265
Pyeongyeon killed Dad!

117
00:10:00,767 --> 00:10:01,768
Dad!

118
00:10:13,613 --> 00:10:14,448
Chris.

119
00:10:17,909 --> 00:10:18,744
Song.

120
00:10:40,599 --> 00:10:42,476
Sorry for just coming in.

121
00:10:43,685 --> 00:10:47,147
But you looked really unwell,
and you were whimpering too.

122
00:10:58,533 --> 00:10:59,701
Chris.

123
00:11:00,619 --> 00:11:02,579
Are you really okay?

124
00:11:03,580 --> 00:11:06,792
You collapsed yesterday,
and you look really exhausted.

125
00:11:08,043 --> 00:11:10,379
Why don't we go to a hospital and--

126
00:11:10,462 --> 00:11:11,296
Song.

127
00:11:12,839 --> 00:11:13,882
I'm fine.

128
00:11:17,386 --> 00:11:19,679
It happens
when I stay in the kitchen too long

129
00:11:20,806 --> 00:11:21,973
under a lot of stress.

130
00:11:23,850 --> 00:11:25,435
You don't have to worry.

131
00:11:25,519 --> 00:11:26,645
It's nothing serious.

132
00:11:29,064 --> 00:11:30,148
I just need to rest a bit.

133
00:11:30,690 --> 00:11:31,525
Just a little bit.

134
00:11:32,984 --> 00:11:33,819
Chris.

135
00:11:34,486 --> 00:11:36,780
Come on, let me take you to a hospital.

136
00:11:37,948 --> 00:11:39,616
You seem to have a slight fever too.

137
00:11:40,450 --> 00:11:43,829
Let's ask a doctor
whether this is serious or not--

138
00:11:43,912 --> 00:11:45,705
I told you I'm fine.

139
00:11:50,127 --> 00:11:51,837
Please, let's just go.

140
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:53,547
I'm worried about you--

141
00:11:53,630 --> 00:11:55,006
Song, please!

142
00:12:18,113 --> 00:12:19,281
Sorry.

143
00:12:21,366 --> 00:12:22,909
I'm sorry for making you worry.

144
00:12:25,078 --> 00:12:26,121
But…

145
00:12:27,205 --> 00:12:28,290
I'm okay.

146
00:12:38,425 --> 00:12:39,593
Hey.

147
00:12:40,177 --> 00:12:41,261
Trust me.

148
00:12:42,095 --> 00:12:43,346
I'm really okay.

149
00:12:47,601 --> 00:12:48,810
We should practice.

150
00:12:49,436 --> 00:12:51,897
Go downstairs and get ready.
I'll join you soon.

151
00:12:59,863 --> 00:13:01,781
ELEMENTARY SCHOOL, MIDDLE SCHOOL,
COLLEGE GRADUATION, CHEF DAYS…

152
00:13:03,867 --> 00:13:05,619
This is his birth father.

153
00:13:06,828 --> 00:13:10,040
The white man in this photo
is his adoptive father.

154
00:13:10,123 --> 00:13:14,753
His adoptive father owned the restaurant
Kang San, his birth father, worked at.

155
00:13:14,836 --> 00:13:17,005
They must've been very close,

156
00:13:17,088 --> 00:13:20,634
seeing how he adopted Kang Han
after Kang San died.

157
00:13:21,718 --> 00:13:24,137
The boy didn't even go to school
before he was adopted.

158
00:13:24,221 --> 00:13:25,514
He was homeschooled.

159
00:13:25,597 --> 00:13:28,475
He was tutored in secret
by people from Korean churches, no less,

160
00:13:29,267 --> 00:13:30,143
so there are no records.

161
00:13:30,810 --> 00:13:33,855
According to one tutor
we barely managed to track down,

162
00:13:34,814 --> 00:13:35,649
the boy

163
00:13:36,441 --> 00:13:38,235
was called "Honey" at home.

164
00:13:39,027 --> 00:13:40,153
His Korean name is "Han."

165
00:13:40,237 --> 00:13:42,197
Americans pronounced it closer to "Ha-ni."

166
00:13:42,781 --> 00:13:43,615
"Ha-ni."

167
00:13:44,366 --> 00:13:45,200
"Honey."

168
00:13:45,951 --> 00:13:48,078
And that name stuck.

169
00:13:48,161 --> 00:13:49,412
"Honey"?

170
00:13:50,997 --> 00:13:52,374
That sounds like a girl's name.

171
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:54,543
Hold on.

172
00:13:55,126 --> 00:13:58,588
When I lived in the US,
the family across the street had a dog.

173
00:13:59,256 --> 00:14:00,966
It was called Honey too.

174
00:14:01,049 --> 00:14:05,595
The darn family's dad, mom, and kids
would never stop yelling "Honey."

175
00:14:05,679 --> 00:14:06,680
You know what, Hyeong-koo?

176
00:14:08,515 --> 00:14:09,724
Unlike its adorable name,

177
00:14:09,808 --> 00:14:14,396
the mutt was so darn ugly.

178
00:14:16,565 --> 00:14:17,941
Is that why they named it that?

179
00:14:18,650 --> 00:14:20,193
So at least it had a pretty name?

180
00:14:20,777 --> 00:14:22,404
Well, I guess so?

181
00:14:24,573 --> 00:14:27,242
He moved all over the place.

182
00:14:27,325 --> 00:14:30,203
That's why they were
so impossible to find.

183
00:14:30,996 --> 00:14:33,206
Man, these tenacious people…

184
00:14:44,718 --> 00:14:47,512
I thought he'd died then.

185
00:14:48,263 --> 00:14:49,806
Turns out, he grew up
to be a fine young man.

186
00:14:52,058 --> 00:14:52,892
How interesting.

187
00:14:54,436 --> 00:14:56,021
I can't believe we meet again like this.

188
00:14:57,897 --> 00:14:59,649
Guess it pays to live long enough.

189
00:15:11,620 --> 00:15:12,454
Hold on.

190
00:15:13,496 --> 00:15:14,956
Play from where they make eye contact.

191
00:15:20,420 --> 00:15:21,379
Pause there.

192
00:15:23,882 --> 00:15:25,175
Play.

193
00:15:28,553 --> 00:15:30,722
Go back to where Chris starts to talk.

194
00:15:30,805 --> 00:15:31,973
In slo-mo, please.

195
00:15:42,108 --> 00:15:43,193
What do you think he's saying?

196
00:15:45,070 --> 00:15:47,656
It's not a friendly conversation, is it?

197
00:15:47,739 --> 00:15:49,115
-No.
-No.

198
00:15:49,866 --> 00:15:51,034
Judging by their expressions,

199
00:15:51,117 --> 00:15:53,578
it seems like
they're exchanging unpleasant words.

200
00:15:53,662 --> 00:15:54,663
Yeah.

201
00:15:56,122 --> 00:15:57,207
It's weird though.

202
00:15:57,290 --> 00:15:58,833
I get the feeling

203
00:15:58,917 --> 00:16:01,336
that they've known each other
for a long time.

204
00:16:01,878 --> 00:16:03,838
Oh, you're right.

205
00:16:03,922 --> 00:16:07,258
It seems like they can
keep the conversation going.

206
00:16:34,994 --> 00:16:36,413
SEA SALT

207
00:18:39,369 --> 00:18:41,621
-Hey.
-Hey. What's up?

208
00:18:42,121 --> 00:18:43,873
I was passing by and decided to come in.

209
00:18:43,957 --> 00:18:46,209
I didn't know Korea had
so many traditional liquors.

210
00:18:47,418 --> 00:18:50,088
You're right. Who knew they had so many?

211
00:18:52,882 --> 00:18:54,217
I don't know any of them.

212
00:18:55,385 --> 00:18:57,178
I've heard of soju and makgeolli,

213
00:18:57,262 --> 00:18:59,097
but leegangju, gamhongnoju,

214
00:18:59,180 --> 00:19:02,350
Jindo hongju, Hansan sogokju,
munbaeju, omegisul…

215
00:19:03,184 --> 00:19:04,519
There are so many different kinds.

216
00:19:05,645 --> 00:19:07,063
Korea's the land of liquor.

217
00:19:11,276 --> 00:19:12,652
This one's over 40% alcohol.

218
00:19:12,735 --> 00:19:13,987
Yeah, it is.

219
00:19:23,705 --> 00:19:25,456
Are you buying all that?

220
00:19:29,377 --> 00:19:32,922
I'm going to taste them all with my team
over some Korean food tonight.

221
00:19:35,008 --> 00:19:36,175
Look.

222
00:19:36,259 --> 00:19:41,472
This is the most popular
food delivery app in Korea.

223
00:19:41,556 --> 00:19:42,974
They've got everything.

224
00:19:43,057 --> 00:19:44,392
It's awesome.

225
00:19:44,475 --> 00:19:45,685
-Awesome?
-Yeah.

226
00:19:49,063 --> 00:19:50,857
Oh, this looks good.

227
00:19:53,943 --> 00:19:55,570
Would you like to join us?

228
00:19:58,448 --> 00:19:59,657
Let's try them together.

229
00:19:59,741 --> 00:20:01,910
Korean delivery food
and traditional liquor.

230
00:20:10,877 --> 00:20:11,836
A bit harder.

231
00:20:11,920 --> 00:20:14,464
Remember what I said about
beating it enough to properly aerate it?

232
00:20:31,356 --> 00:20:34,400
It's okay to stir like this now
because it's not fully cooked yet.

233
00:20:35,860 --> 00:20:38,655
Don't forget to cook it slowly
over low heat.

234
00:20:41,199 --> 00:20:43,368
Scrape around the edges of the pan
as you tilt it.

235
00:20:45,161 --> 00:20:46,412
Now, you try it.

236
00:20:50,291 --> 00:20:52,043
No, no. Not that spot.

237
00:20:52,126 --> 00:20:53,544
You can't touch that part now.

238
00:20:54,128 --> 00:20:56,756
That part is cooked,
so touching it will ruin the shape.

239
00:20:58,132 --> 00:20:59,384
Let's sprinkle some cheese now.

240
00:21:02,303 --> 00:21:04,847
It cooks in the blink of an eye now,
so you must be quick.

241
00:21:05,556 --> 00:21:06,516
Okay?

242
00:21:07,558 --> 00:21:09,686
Tilt the pan and shake it a little.

243
00:21:09,769 --> 00:21:11,896
Fold it over from the outside in.

244
00:21:11,980 --> 00:21:12,981
Okay.

245
00:21:13,940 --> 00:21:15,149
Not like that. Bit by bit.

246
00:21:15,650 --> 00:21:16,859
Do it gently…

247
00:21:20,154 --> 00:21:21,239
Let's try again.

248
00:21:39,382 --> 00:21:40,216
Again.

249
00:21:41,384 --> 00:21:42,760
It's overcooked.

250
00:21:47,390 --> 00:21:48,599
I told you to tilt the pan.

251
00:21:49,350 --> 00:21:50,560
Again.

252
00:21:57,191 --> 00:21:58,234
Stop.

253
00:22:04,490 --> 00:22:06,576
-Again.
-Darn it.

254
00:22:20,548 --> 00:22:21,549
Here, let me.

255
00:22:35,146 --> 00:22:36,230
Okay.

256
00:22:47,784 --> 00:22:49,035
It's tough, isn't it?

257
00:22:49,118 --> 00:22:50,620
An omelet looks easy to make,

258
00:22:50,703 --> 00:22:52,205
but it's tricky to get it right.

259
00:22:52,830 --> 00:22:54,457
You're using a spatula now,

260
00:22:54,540 --> 00:22:56,459
but you have to be able to do it
with chopsticks later on.

261
00:22:57,877 --> 00:22:58,836
Remember.

262
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:00,713
Practice is everything.

263
00:23:08,888 --> 00:23:10,014
Song.

264
00:23:10,098 --> 00:23:11,557
Don't be so disappointed.

265
00:23:12,809 --> 00:23:15,186
Making an omelet is harder than you think.

266
00:23:15,269 --> 00:23:16,312
At first, everyone--

267
00:23:21,025 --> 00:23:22,193
Sorry.

268
00:23:22,276 --> 00:23:23,653
I need some fresh air.

269
00:24:05,278 --> 00:24:08,948
The women in my family are terrible cooks.

270
00:24:11,659 --> 00:24:13,452
My mom told me so when I was young.

271
00:24:14,203 --> 00:24:16,330
She wasn't good at cooking either.

272
00:24:21,460 --> 00:24:26,465
Most of the food I remember until this day
was made by my dad.

273
00:24:27,717 --> 00:24:29,427
Not just Western cuisine.

274
00:24:30,386 --> 00:24:33,264
Soybean paste stew, braised kimchi,

275
00:24:33,347 --> 00:24:35,516
spicy stir-fried pork, bibimbap,

276
00:24:36,350 --> 00:24:37,435
seasoned greens…

277
00:24:38,728 --> 00:24:39,729
Well, everything.

278
00:24:43,524 --> 00:24:45,193
I probably take after my mom,

279
00:24:45,818 --> 00:24:47,820
seeing how I'm a terrible cook.

280
00:24:51,908 --> 00:24:52,825
On top of that,

281
00:24:54,076 --> 00:24:56,412
I shouldn't even be allowed in a kitchen.

282
00:24:58,873 --> 00:25:02,501
Apparently, a ghost on my shoulder
loses its mind when it sees fire.

283
00:25:07,089 --> 00:25:10,509
I was told others would get hurt
if I were in a kitchen…

284
00:25:12,428 --> 00:25:13,888
because I was cursed.

285
00:25:17,558 --> 00:25:19,060
It sounds stupid, doesn't it?

286
00:25:20,645 --> 00:25:21,646
But…

287
00:25:23,648 --> 00:25:25,942
people actually did get hurt.

288
00:25:27,735 --> 00:25:28,903
Even my dad.

289
00:25:33,074 --> 00:25:34,075
Chris.

290
00:25:38,454 --> 00:25:40,456
I'm no good for this restaurant.

291
00:25:41,207 --> 00:25:42,708
I'm only getting in the way.

292
00:25:45,586 --> 00:25:47,546
No matter how hard you try,

293
00:25:50,174 --> 00:25:51,509
I won't improve.

294
00:25:55,513 --> 00:25:58,140
I don't deserve to be on the team.

295
00:26:04,230 --> 00:26:05,523
If someone

296
00:26:06,857 --> 00:26:08,317
were to get hurt because of me…

297
00:26:12,113 --> 00:26:14,865
If someone were to actually get hurt
because of me…

298
00:26:29,338 --> 00:26:30,881
I know how you feel.

299
00:26:33,509 --> 00:26:34,885
I also know that nothing I say

300
00:26:35,678 --> 00:26:37,221
will really reach you.

301
00:26:40,975 --> 00:26:42,435
That's how I felt at first.

302
00:26:47,940 --> 00:26:48,858
Song.

303
00:26:50,443 --> 00:26:54,322
Cooking is science, not a superstition.

304
00:26:55,114 --> 00:26:57,116
There's no such thing as ghosts or curses.

305
00:26:57,992 --> 00:27:00,453
There's only repetition and practice.

306
00:27:01,037 --> 00:27:01,871
Okay?

307
00:27:06,083 --> 00:27:08,252
No matter what you've been through

308
00:27:09,378 --> 00:27:10,755
or what anyone has told you,

309
00:27:11,630 --> 00:27:12,965
just remember this one thing.

310
00:27:16,552 --> 00:27:18,804
Don't ever try to run away from or forget

311
00:27:19,638 --> 00:27:22,558
whatever happens in your life.

312
00:27:26,354 --> 00:27:27,396
A life you turn away from

313
00:27:28,105 --> 00:27:31,317
can never truly be yours.

314
00:27:39,825 --> 00:27:41,077
I won't give up

315
00:27:42,078 --> 00:27:45,664
on you, Famiglia, or this competition.

316
00:27:50,961 --> 00:27:52,129
So, Song.

317
00:27:52,922 --> 00:27:54,048
Don't ever let go.

318
00:27:54,799 --> 00:27:56,425
Let's not give up.

319
00:28:03,724 --> 00:28:04,683
Come on.

320
00:28:08,646 --> 00:28:10,356
To make a delicious omelet,

321
00:28:10,940 --> 00:28:12,650
you must beat the eggs well.

322
00:28:12,733 --> 00:28:15,027
It's also important to season them
before cooking them.

323
00:28:15,528 --> 00:28:19,156
What matters most is controlling the heat
and knowing when to roll.

324
00:28:19,240 --> 00:28:21,659
Mess those up, and nothing can save you.

325
00:28:23,994 --> 00:28:25,413
The rest is time.

326
00:28:25,496 --> 00:28:26,705
Time will take care of it.

327
00:28:27,581 --> 00:28:31,377
We'll make a couple more omelets,
and then begin palate training.

328
00:28:34,880 --> 00:28:35,714
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026
ROUND 3

329
00:28:35,798 --> 00:28:37,883
HAEON COUNTY,
THE SOUTHERNMOST TIP OF KOREA

330
00:28:42,179 --> 00:28:43,389
HAEON ELEMENTARY SCHOOL

331
00:28:46,934 --> 00:28:48,144
YOU ARE MY DESTINY

332
00:28:48,227 --> 00:28:49,562
ROUND 3
WITH HAEON ELEMENTARY SCHOOL GRADUATES

333
00:28:49,645 --> 00:28:51,105
OUTDOOR STAGE
A SPECIAL KITCHEN BUILT ON THE FIELD

334
00:28:51,188 --> 00:28:52,857
COLLABORATIVE WORK

335
00:28:52,940 --> 00:28:54,442
RANDOM DRAW

336
00:28:59,530 --> 00:29:00,990
At first,

337
00:29:01,073 --> 00:29:03,826
I thought the theme sounded dumb.

338
00:29:04,326 --> 00:29:05,995
"You Are My Destiny."

339
00:29:06,662 --> 00:29:08,581
That thing called destiny.

340
00:29:11,625 --> 00:29:13,127
It's usually used

341
00:29:13,210 --> 00:29:16,172
to describe the connections
between married couples or their children.

342
00:29:16,797 --> 00:29:17,798
But I don't see

343
00:29:18,591 --> 00:29:22,678
how food could ever have
such a strong connection with anyone.

344
00:29:22,761 --> 00:29:25,556
Of course, I know very little about food,

345
00:29:25,639 --> 00:29:27,683
but those who devote
their whole lives to it…

346
00:29:27,766 --> 00:29:28,851
Well, your husband, for one.

347
00:29:34,482 --> 00:29:37,443
He was a renowned Korean cuisine chef.

348
00:29:37,943 --> 00:29:39,320
What was his name again?

349
00:29:40,070 --> 00:29:42,865
Was it Kang San? Wasn't your son…

350
00:29:47,912 --> 00:29:49,079
I'm sorry.

351
00:29:49,663 --> 00:29:51,081
I shouldn't have brought up the past.

352
00:29:51,165 --> 00:29:54,335
Having a good memory
can cause trouble sometimes.

353
00:29:54,835 --> 00:29:56,378
It's a taboo topic at Pyeongyeon.

354
00:29:57,046 --> 00:29:58,672
Maybe I should stitch up my darn lips.

355
00:30:02,760 --> 00:30:04,303
I'VE ATTACHED MATERIALS ON CHRIS K.
RESTAURANTS

356
00:30:04,386 --> 00:30:05,554
RESTAURANTS WITH CHRIS K.
FULLY BOOKED 6 MONTHS AHEAD

357
00:30:09,350 --> 00:30:10,726
IN NEW YORK'S FINE-DINING INDUSTRY,
CHRIS K…

358
00:30:12,394 --> 00:30:13,229
INDUSTRY'S INTEREST

359
00:30:13,312 --> 00:30:14,563
NEW YORK'S CULINARY WORLD
TAKES NOTICE OF AN ASIAN CHEF

360
00:30:16,315 --> 00:30:18,150
SOUS-CHEF MORE FAMOUS THAN THE HEAD CHEF…

361
00:30:19,360 --> 00:30:20,569
ONE BITE, AND I UNDERSTOOD THE HYPE

362
00:30:20,653 --> 00:30:21,862
EVERYTHING ELSE SEEMS BLAND NOW

363
00:30:23,656 --> 00:30:25,157
INTERVIEWS, RESTAURANT REVIEWS,
ACADEMIC RECORDS

364
00:30:34,208 --> 00:30:36,293
NO RECORDS FROM BEFORE HIGH SCHOOL
COULD BE FOUND

365
00:30:47,555 --> 00:30:48,430
Level three.

366
00:30:49,390 --> 00:30:50,224
No.

367
00:30:51,350 --> 00:30:52,643
It's not level four either.

368
00:30:53,310 --> 00:30:55,479
It's between level three and four.

369
00:30:56,939 --> 00:30:58,315
Is it level three

370
00:30:59,024 --> 00:31:01,235
with just an extra pinch of salt?

371
00:31:06,991 --> 00:31:08,367
-Chris.
-Huh?

372
00:31:08,450 --> 00:31:09,785
What level is it?

373
00:31:11,036 --> 00:31:11,870
You're right.

374
00:31:12,830 --> 00:31:14,373
It's between level three and four.

375
00:31:15,040 --> 00:31:17,167
I added 50 mg to the level 3 solution.

376
00:31:18,085 --> 00:31:20,671
-How did you--
-Yes!

377
00:31:21,171 --> 00:31:22,590
I knew it.

378
00:31:22,673 --> 00:31:25,009
You set a trap to fool me, huh?
But I got it.

379
00:31:25,092 --> 00:31:26,844
I got them all.

380
00:31:31,307 --> 00:31:32,474
This is crazy.

381
00:31:33,726 --> 00:31:34,560
Song.

382
00:31:35,311 --> 00:31:36,228
Yes?

383
00:31:36,312 --> 00:31:38,439
You're the first person ever
to get all ten levels right

384
00:31:39,273 --> 00:31:41,442
and not fall for my trap.

385
00:31:43,360 --> 00:31:44,361
This is…

386
00:31:48,032 --> 00:31:48,866
Song.

387
00:31:49,825 --> 00:31:51,535
You have an amazing talent.

388
00:31:52,077 --> 00:31:53,912
Something even chefs rarely have.

389
00:31:56,874 --> 00:31:58,375
Let's practice a bit more.

390
00:31:58,459 --> 00:32:00,294
This time, I'll teach you about
the flavors and aromas of herbs.

391
00:32:00,878 --> 00:32:01,837
Wait here.

392
00:32:12,681 --> 00:32:13,557
Yes, ma'am.

393
00:32:14,808 --> 00:32:17,186
Did I catch you at a bad time?
Can we talk?

394
00:32:17,269 --> 00:32:18,937
Yes, we can.

395
00:32:19,021 --> 00:32:20,564
I just finished serving lunch.

396
00:32:21,565 --> 00:32:22,650
I see.

397
00:32:28,364 --> 00:32:30,449
Is something wrong?

398
00:32:33,327 --> 00:32:34,995
Hwang returned from their trip

399
00:32:35,079 --> 00:32:37,831
and brought back some excellent
barley-aged dried yellow croakers.

400
00:32:39,208 --> 00:32:40,542
Can you come over in the evening?

401
00:32:40,626 --> 00:32:43,253
We haven't had dinner together in a while.

402
00:32:45,673 --> 00:32:47,841
I already called
to apologize to Reporter Park.

403
00:32:47,925 --> 00:32:49,843
Start with the pre-dinner cocktail.

404
00:32:49,927 --> 00:32:52,262
-Serve a bottle of champagne on us.
-Yes, Chef.

405
00:32:52,346 --> 00:32:55,599
Don't forget to say it's my gift,
and there's no pressure.

406
00:32:55,683 --> 00:32:57,643
-I understand. Goodbye.
-Bye.

407
00:34:07,713 --> 00:34:09,298
Isabel. You're here.

408
00:34:30,194 --> 00:34:31,487
I invited her.

409
00:34:32,154 --> 00:34:33,989
She's been working hard lately.

410
00:34:35,115 --> 00:34:37,701
Hasn't it been a while
since you both had a home-cooked meal?

411
00:34:39,161 --> 00:34:39,995
It has.

412
00:34:41,079 --> 00:34:42,039
Come on in.

413
00:34:48,003 --> 00:34:50,047
I'm not sure if it's to your taste.

414
00:34:50,589 --> 00:34:51,924
It's seasoned to my old taste buds.

415
00:34:52,508 --> 00:34:54,718
Not at all. It's delicious, ma'am.

416
00:34:55,636 --> 00:34:57,262
I'm enjoying it too.

417
00:34:57,346 --> 00:34:58,639
I'm glad.

418
00:34:58,722 --> 00:35:00,682
I was worried you wouldn't like it.

419
00:35:01,266 --> 00:35:02,684
Take your time and eat your fill.

420
00:35:03,769 --> 00:35:04,686
Yes, ma'am.

421
00:35:07,064 --> 00:35:09,650
You can drop the honorific
when you're here.

422
00:35:11,777 --> 00:35:12,611
Pardon?

423
00:35:16,490 --> 00:35:17,741
I understand, ma'am.

424
00:35:18,992 --> 00:35:19,868
I mean…

425
00:35:21,703 --> 00:35:22,955
I understand.

426
00:35:29,461 --> 00:35:30,629
As I get older,

427
00:35:31,463 --> 00:35:33,715
my palate seems to be reverting
to that of a child.

428
00:35:34,883 --> 00:35:36,093
I used to prefer

429
00:35:36,677 --> 00:35:39,346
to eat this fish as it was,
without extra seasoning.

430
00:35:39,429 --> 00:35:40,639
But as the years go by,

431
00:35:40,722 --> 00:35:44,601
I find myself preferring it
with seasoned gochujang sauce.

432
00:35:46,478 --> 00:35:49,106
Am I missing my childhood
when I preferred strong flavors?

433
00:35:49,189 --> 00:35:51,275
Or have my taste buds gotten dull,

434
00:35:51,358 --> 00:35:53,610
making me crave stronger kicks?

435
00:35:58,407 --> 00:36:00,033
"You Are My Destiny."

436
00:36:01,493 --> 00:36:02,744
When I was young,

437
00:36:04,162 --> 00:36:06,665
I believed my destiny
was to realize my dream.

438
00:36:07,833 --> 00:36:09,042
Then my belief changed,

439
00:36:09,877 --> 00:36:11,712
shifting the focus to my family.

440
00:36:14,047 --> 00:36:16,425
Now that I've lost
both my dream and my family…

441
00:36:19,845 --> 00:36:21,388
I wonder what my destiny is.

442
00:36:32,399 --> 00:36:33,317
Ma'am.

443
00:36:37,070 --> 00:36:41,074
Didn't you spend a part of your childhood
in Haeon County?

444
00:36:41,700 --> 00:36:44,286
And you graduated
from Haeon Elementary School,

445
00:36:44,369 --> 00:36:45,579
where the upcoming round will be held.

446
00:36:47,623 --> 00:36:48,916
I did.

447
00:36:55,005 --> 00:36:56,173
In that case,

448
00:36:57,799 --> 00:37:00,802
why don't you take part in the next round
as a judge?

449
00:37:06,642 --> 00:37:08,143
As a judge?

450
00:37:24,701 --> 00:37:26,203
When do you fly out?

451
00:37:27,496 --> 00:37:29,122
In the morning, the day after tomorrow.

452
00:37:32,459 --> 00:37:35,629
I got to travel around Korea
thanks to being eliminated early.

453
00:37:36,338 --> 00:37:38,840
I feel like
I got to know the country better.

454
00:37:39,800 --> 00:37:42,302
Though, of course,
there's still more to learn.

455
00:37:44,137 --> 00:37:48,558
I'm jealous that you got to relax
and travel the country a bit.

456
00:37:49,768 --> 00:37:51,728
I wish I got eliminated early.

457
00:37:58,777 --> 00:38:00,445
How do you like the Korean liquor?

458
00:38:02,072 --> 00:38:03,740
Harsh, but…

459
00:38:04,950 --> 00:38:06,076
It's nicer than I thought.

460
00:38:06,618 --> 00:38:08,912
They're mostly made
from fermented grains like rice,

461
00:38:08,996 --> 00:38:11,289
so they don't have particularly
complex aromas or bouquets.

462
00:38:11,999 --> 00:38:12,833
How should I put this?

463
00:38:16,086 --> 00:38:17,295
They taste really clean,

464
00:38:18,296 --> 00:38:21,758
like cutting through something
with a sharp blade.

465
00:38:23,760 --> 00:38:25,762
It completely resets your palate.

466
00:38:26,680 --> 00:38:27,764
That's my take on it.

467
00:38:37,399 --> 00:38:38,483
Holy cow.

468
00:38:41,486 --> 00:38:43,488
-It is harsh.
-It is.

469
00:38:45,532 --> 00:38:46,783
-You okay?
-Yeah.

470
00:38:59,004 --> 00:39:02,382
If you ever find yourself in the US,

471
00:39:03,300 --> 00:39:04,342
come to Charleston.

472
00:39:05,802 --> 00:39:07,387
I promise I will.

473
00:39:11,683 --> 00:39:12,517
Joshua.

474
00:39:14,853 --> 00:39:18,190
I really hope you win this competition.

475
00:39:19,232 --> 00:39:20,525
I mean it.

476
00:39:22,152 --> 00:39:24,780
And whatever you came here to find,

477
00:39:27,032 --> 00:39:28,533
I hope you find it.

478
00:40:14,454 --> 00:40:16,331
What are you doing
so early in the morning?

479
00:40:17,207 --> 00:40:18,250
Hi, Song.

480
00:40:20,001 --> 00:40:22,879
I'm going to a nearby mountain

481
00:40:23,463 --> 00:40:24,673
and dropping by a campground.

482
00:40:25,465 --> 00:40:27,676
You're going camping? By yourself?

483
00:40:28,635 --> 00:40:32,013
You have time for that
during a competition?

484
00:40:33,932 --> 00:40:35,183
It's our day off.

485
00:40:35,267 --> 00:40:37,519
I can afford to go clear my head, no?

486
00:40:38,395 --> 00:40:42,065
Besides, we'll be away for five whole days
for the upcoming round.

487
00:40:43,692 --> 00:40:45,360
Don't worry. I'll be back by dawn.

488
00:40:48,363 --> 00:40:51,241
You can take a break from practicing too.

489
00:40:54,244 --> 00:40:55,078
Okay.

490
00:41:12,345 --> 00:41:13,471
-Can I--
-Do you want to--

491
00:41:22,480 --> 00:41:25,483
Can I come with you?

492
00:41:27,736 --> 00:41:31,656
Well, I have nothing to do here on my own,

493
00:41:31,740 --> 00:41:33,950
and I could use some fresh air.

494
00:41:35,327 --> 00:41:38,622
I can practice cooking at the campground,

495
00:41:39,289 --> 00:41:42,000
and it'd be easier
to get there with my truck.

496
00:41:42,542 --> 00:41:43,752
Why not, right?

497
00:41:48,173 --> 00:41:49,341
All right.

498
00:42:02,103 --> 00:42:03,355
Chris.

499
00:42:04,272 --> 00:42:06,816
What happened to camping?
Why are we hiking?

500
00:43:18,596 --> 00:43:19,514
All done.

501
00:43:23,310 --> 00:43:24,269
Let's go.

502
00:43:36,072 --> 00:43:36,906
Take my hand.

503
00:43:40,160 --> 00:43:41,494
It'll be easier if you do.

504
00:43:43,663 --> 00:43:44,497
Come on.

505
00:43:49,419 --> 00:43:50,754
Come on, let's get going.

506
00:44:33,755 --> 00:44:34,714
What's this?

507
00:44:34,798 --> 00:44:36,758
Why is it so numbingly bitter?

508
00:44:39,636 --> 00:44:40,470
Did you swallow it?

509
00:44:41,346 --> 00:44:43,181
You can't eat that raw.

510
00:44:45,934 --> 00:44:47,519
Hurry up and rinse your mouth out.

511
00:44:55,276 --> 00:44:56,694
What was that?

512
00:44:56,778 --> 00:44:59,364
-Why does it taste like that?
-It's a poisonous plant.

513
00:44:59,447 --> 00:45:01,241
What? Poisonous?

514
00:45:01,324 --> 00:45:02,617
Don't worry.

515
00:45:03,159 --> 00:45:05,286
You didn't eat enough
for it to be a serious problem.

516
00:45:06,579 --> 00:45:08,081
You need to be careful though.

517
00:45:08,873 --> 00:45:11,751
A piece the size of your thumb
could make you go blind.

518
00:45:13,044 --> 00:45:15,588
Why on earth
do you gather poisonous plants?

519
00:45:15,672 --> 00:45:17,966
What if someone eats it by mistake
and becomes ill or worse?

520
00:45:18,842 --> 00:45:19,676
By any chance,

521
00:45:20,468 --> 00:45:22,887
are you thinking of doing something bad?

522
00:45:24,305 --> 00:45:25,348
What?

523
00:45:27,225 --> 00:45:29,394
Don't tell me these are all poisonous too.

524
00:45:30,395 --> 00:45:32,355
These two aren't.

525
00:45:32,439 --> 00:45:33,857
Then the rest are?

526
00:45:35,859 --> 00:45:37,902
Have you lost it?
Are you trying to get someone killed?

527
00:45:37,986 --> 00:45:41,281
You can't take
this many dangerous plants with you!

528
00:45:41,906 --> 00:45:43,867
If I store them properly--

529
00:45:43,950 --> 00:45:45,285
Even so.

530
00:45:45,910 --> 00:45:47,954
You can't take all this dangerous stuff.

531
00:45:48,037 --> 00:45:49,122
I'm going to throw them all away.

532
00:45:49,205 --> 00:45:51,207
No, no. You can't. I need them all.

533
00:45:51,291 --> 00:45:52,917
Why would you need something so dangerous?

534
00:45:53,001 --> 00:45:53,835
Hand them over.

535
00:45:53,918 --> 00:45:55,879
It took me ages to collect all this.

536
00:45:55,962 --> 00:45:58,214
-Give it to me. It's dangerous.
-No.

537
00:45:58,298 --> 00:46:00,758
-You said so yourself. They're poisonous!
-No. No!

538
00:46:01,342 --> 00:46:02,677
-Chris!
-No!

539
00:47:10,078 --> 00:47:11,204
This idiot prepared pufferfish

540
00:47:11,287 --> 00:47:13,164
and didn't make the cut
in the second round.

541
00:47:22,131 --> 00:47:24,842
Yeah, her!
It was such a shame to lose her!

542
00:47:24,926 --> 00:47:25,760
Look, Hyeong-koo.

543
00:47:25,843 --> 00:47:28,680
A pretty face and a sexy body.

544
00:47:28,763 --> 00:47:30,223
People liked her a lot.

545
00:47:32,100 --> 00:47:33,935
She got eliminated way too early.

546
00:47:39,566 --> 00:47:41,901
Let me see.

547
00:47:41,985 --> 00:47:43,570
Let's see.

548
00:47:47,115 --> 00:47:48,533
If he wins,

549
00:47:48,616 --> 00:47:50,743
he can't take over the company.

550
00:47:53,746 --> 00:47:56,666
If he wins, people will accuse her
of rigging the competition

551
00:47:56,749 --> 00:47:58,835
to make her secret biological son win.

552
00:48:01,004 --> 00:48:02,130
Checkmate.

553
00:48:02,714 --> 00:48:03,590
It's checkmate.

554
00:48:03,673 --> 00:48:04,924
What now?

555
00:48:05,550 --> 00:48:06,509
Who should win?

556
00:48:06,593 --> 00:48:09,721
Eeny, meeny, miny, moe.

557
00:48:09,804 --> 00:48:12,140
It's giving me a headache!

558
00:48:12,223 --> 00:48:13,600
I should just…

559
00:48:17,270 --> 00:48:18,104
Moon Hae-seon.

560
00:48:18,896 --> 00:48:20,690
Yeah, you. Moon Hae-seon!

561
00:48:27,947 --> 00:48:30,325
You. I don't like you.

562
00:48:30,408 --> 00:48:31,909
I really don't like you.

563
00:48:32,952 --> 00:48:37,457
But I should let you live
since you're useful.

564
00:48:39,417 --> 00:48:40,585
Forget all of them!

565
00:48:40,668 --> 00:48:43,254
I hate this old French man!

566
00:48:43,338 --> 00:48:45,298
He looks like he's up to something.

567
00:48:45,381 --> 00:48:47,508
He gives off this weird, iffy vibe

568
00:48:47,592 --> 00:48:50,470
that he knows something
but pretends not to!

569
00:48:50,553 --> 00:48:53,014
He looks like a damn hound.

570
00:48:53,598 --> 00:48:54,724
Damn it!

571
00:48:54,807 --> 00:48:57,101
He wears a weird cologne
that smells like horse manure.

572
00:49:01,689 --> 00:49:02,774
The third round has started.

573
00:49:04,108 --> 00:49:05,401
Shall we warm up too?

574
00:49:06,235 --> 00:49:07,070
Yes, ma'am.

575
00:49:07,153 --> 00:49:08,404
What should we do first?

576
00:49:15,495 --> 00:49:16,996
What should we give him?

577
00:49:19,332 --> 00:49:21,668
I wasn't born with natural talent

578
00:49:22,460 --> 00:49:23,836
or exceptional instincts.

579
00:49:25,546 --> 00:49:27,715
Everything was a new challenge.

580
00:49:29,217 --> 00:49:30,968
I had to learn everything from scratch.

581
00:49:31,052 --> 00:49:32,929
I tried and faced countless failures.

582
00:49:34,222 --> 00:49:35,848
Then I started over again.

583
00:49:37,433 --> 00:49:38,393
And failed again.

584
00:49:42,563 --> 00:49:43,731
But people

585
00:49:44,982 --> 00:49:46,359
are never really interested

586
00:49:47,568 --> 00:49:49,153
in the process.

587
00:50:02,125 --> 00:50:03,126
Can I…

588
00:50:04,377 --> 00:50:05,795
ask you something?

589
00:50:07,672 --> 00:50:11,342
What does this competition mean to you?

590
00:50:12,510 --> 00:50:18,349
You never explained
why you suddenly changed your mind

591
00:50:18,433 --> 00:50:20,017
and decided to join the competition.

592
00:50:20,101 --> 00:50:22,520
Why did you suddenly change your mind?

593
00:50:35,533 --> 00:50:37,744
Remember the day you came to Famiglia?

594
00:50:38,619 --> 00:50:40,663
You said in the kitchen

595
00:50:41,289 --> 00:50:45,793
that cooking isn't just about
serving good food.

596
00:50:47,420 --> 00:50:50,381
That food can bring back
someone's precious memories,

597
00:50:51,507 --> 00:50:52,717
and for some,

598
00:50:54,343 --> 00:50:56,512
it can give them courage to live on.

599
00:51:01,684 --> 00:51:03,394
That's what Famiglia

600
00:51:04,729 --> 00:51:07,356
and Famiglia's food mean to me.

601
00:51:09,442 --> 00:51:10,485
That's why…

602
00:51:12,153 --> 00:51:13,321
I want to win

603
00:51:14,322 --> 00:51:15,782
this competition, no matter what.

604
00:51:18,159 --> 00:51:19,243
Even though

605
00:51:20,077 --> 00:51:23,039
I don't have what it takes
to run the restaurant yet,

606
00:51:23,831 --> 00:51:26,042
and I'm a terrible cook

607
00:51:26,125 --> 00:51:29,378
who has to stay away from the kitchen
because of the curse,

608
00:51:30,338 --> 00:51:31,589
I want to win.

609
00:51:33,424 --> 00:51:36,594
I want to beat Pyeongyeon Group
that killed my dad.

610
00:51:48,815 --> 00:51:50,024
Want to know a secret?

611
00:51:50,650 --> 00:51:51,734
What?

612
00:51:53,569 --> 00:51:54,570
I can only smell

613
00:51:55,655 --> 00:51:59,325
a third of what an ordinary person
can smell.

614
00:52:00,201 --> 00:52:01,118
What?

615
00:52:01,202 --> 00:52:03,037
It's partially genetic.

616
00:52:04,247 --> 00:52:06,165
But it's also because
I ate a poisonous plant as a kid

617
00:52:06,707 --> 00:52:08,501
that made me lose
70% of my sense of smell.

618
00:52:09,794 --> 00:52:10,837
Then…

619
00:52:12,255 --> 00:52:13,297
Then how…

620
00:52:15,132 --> 00:52:18,719
How do you smell ingredients
and season food accurately

621
00:52:18,803 --> 00:52:20,680
with a reduced sense of smell?

622
00:52:23,015 --> 00:52:23,891
I don't know.

623
00:52:24,433 --> 00:52:25,393
What do you think?

624
00:52:29,522 --> 00:52:30,523
Destiny.

625
00:52:35,069 --> 00:52:36,487
Think of it as destiny.

626
00:52:39,782 --> 00:52:42,702
Your inescapable, unavoidable destiny.

627
00:52:47,331 --> 00:52:48,749
The solutions come later.

628
00:52:50,001 --> 00:52:51,335
Whatever they may be.

629
00:52:54,922 --> 00:52:56,257
As long as you believe…

630
00:52:58,342 --> 00:53:01,637
you can shape your destiny.

631
00:53:36,047 --> 00:53:37,798
YOU ARE MY DESTINY
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026

632
00:53:51,854 --> 00:53:54,023
This next round will take place

633
00:53:55,107 --> 00:53:56,192
far down south

634
00:53:57,151 --> 00:53:58,152
in Haeon County.

635
00:54:02,531 --> 00:54:04,659
To make your trip more comfortable,

636
00:54:05,242 --> 00:54:06,994
we prepared three cars,
each in a different color.

637
00:54:07,078 --> 00:54:10,414
Two chefs will share a car.

638
00:54:11,916 --> 00:54:13,376
There's no need to worry.

639
00:54:13,459 --> 00:54:16,796
Your teammates will follow in a bus.

640
00:54:19,298 --> 00:54:24,637
In the car, you will each receive
a different hint about the mission.

641
00:54:26,222 --> 00:54:30,351
Your hint will depend on
the car you choose.

642
00:54:31,394 --> 00:54:33,521
The number you choose and press

643
00:54:33,604 --> 00:54:36,565
is the number of the car you will take.

644
00:54:37,692 --> 00:54:41,278
Each of you drew a slip of paper
when you entered this room.

645
00:54:42,154 --> 00:54:44,031
The number on your slip
determines your order.

646
00:55:10,182 --> 00:55:12,935
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026

647
00:56:18,459 --> 00:56:19,668
There are no cameras here.

648
00:58:41,227 --> 00:58:43,103
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026

649
00:58:50,319 --> 00:58:51,237
Where are we?

650
00:58:52,863 --> 00:58:54,698
-It's an empty lot.
-Did we come to the wrong place?

651
00:58:54,782 --> 00:58:56,951
The two chefs in each car

652
00:58:57,660 --> 00:58:59,578
are teaming up for this round.

653
00:59:02,456 --> 00:59:05,292
You will compete against others as a team.

654
00:59:06,252 --> 00:59:10,506
For this mission,
the two head chefs must work together.

655
00:59:10,589 --> 00:59:13,884
Just like the theme,
you are each other's destiny.

656
00:59:17,263 --> 00:59:18,764
In addition,

657
00:59:18,847 --> 00:59:21,850
each head chef can pick

658
00:59:21,934 --> 00:59:24,270
just one person from their team to join.

659
00:59:25,062 --> 00:59:27,982
Just one person from each team
will be selected.

660
00:59:28,065 --> 00:59:31,151
The one person each head chef picks

661
00:59:31,694 --> 00:59:34,530
will travel to Haeon County
to participate in this round.

662
00:59:34,613 --> 00:59:36,740
What's going on?

663
00:59:38,158 --> 00:59:39,118
What? Just one of us?

664
00:59:42,288 --> 00:59:45,833
Please choose your teammate carefully

665
00:59:45,916 --> 00:59:48,460
and send them a message.

666
00:59:53,465 --> 00:59:57,261
The message will simply say,
"You are my destiny."

667
00:59:57,344 --> 01:00:01,724
Please raise your hand
if you receive that message.

668
01:00:14,486 --> 01:00:15,863
-What?
-She got it.

669
01:00:15,946 --> 01:00:16,989
Isabel?

670
01:00:56,820 --> 01:00:57,821
Please, Isabel.

671
01:00:59,740 --> 01:01:01,575
My sous-chef left.

672
01:01:02,368 --> 01:01:03,911
Can you be my sous-chef?

673
01:01:06,580 --> 01:01:07,998
Please be my teammate

674
01:01:09,792 --> 01:01:11,168
in the remaining rounds.

675
01:01:14,380 --> 01:01:15,631
Excuse me.

676
01:01:40,531 --> 01:01:41,365
Good luck.

677
01:01:51,708 --> 01:01:53,460
Go get them, Chef Park.

678
01:01:55,129 --> 01:01:56,004
You've got this.

679
01:01:57,339 --> 01:01:59,925
Yes, Geon-woo.

680
01:02:00,008 --> 01:02:02,636
Don't worry too much.
Chris will take care of everything.

681
01:02:04,721 --> 01:02:05,556
Sure.

682
01:02:12,938 --> 01:02:13,897
I knew it.

683
01:02:18,569 --> 01:02:19,403
This won't be easy.

684
01:02:58,275 --> 01:02:59,234
No way.

685
01:03:01,820 --> 01:03:02,821
What?

686
01:03:10,078 --> 01:03:11,872
YOU ARE MY DESTINY

687
01:03:11,955 --> 01:03:13,540
CHRIS K.

688
01:04:06,677 --> 01:04:08,679
What on earth are you thinking?

689
01:04:08,762 --> 01:04:09,805
Is he insane?

690
01:04:11,098 --> 01:04:12,558
If we get eliminated because of you two…

691
01:04:13,850 --> 01:04:16,687
Don't worry. I trust Song's instincts.

692
01:04:17,604 --> 01:04:20,524
Are you saying
this round will be broadcast live?

693
01:04:20,607 --> 01:04:21,942
A secret family relationship?

694
01:04:22,025 --> 01:04:23,777
Completely unedited and raw!

695
01:04:23,860 --> 01:04:26,196
The mission tied to this theme is…

696
01:04:26,280 --> 01:04:29,074
My team doesn't make stupid mistakes.

697
01:04:29,157 --> 01:04:30,409
Get out of my sight.

698
01:04:30,492 --> 01:04:31,785
Right now!

699
01:04:31,868 --> 01:04:33,453
This isn't your team.

700
01:04:33,537 --> 01:04:34,496
You little…

701
01:04:35,080 --> 01:04:36,123
It's our team.

702
01:04:41,295 --> 01:04:44,298
Subtitle: Jennifer Jean Lim
